<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Nanjing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> was the peace treaty of 1842 that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>. It ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> to Britain, opened five ports to British trade and residence, and required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to pay a large indemnity. Accounts of the Qing Empire treat it as the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unequal-treaties">unequal treaties</a> that marked the forced opening of China to Western powers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nanjing.jpg" alt="Treaty of Nanjing" width="320" height="120" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Painted by Captain John Platt, Bengal Volunteers. Engraved by John Burnet. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Signing_of_the_Treaty_of_Nanking.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the late 18th and early 19th centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> ran a growing trade deficit with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, since it had nothing to offer that matched its imports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>. Opium grown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> was auctioned to merchants who sold it to Chinese smugglers, although Chinese law forbade its import and sale. When Lin Zexu seized the opium and ordered its destruction at Humen, Britain demanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> and then declared what became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a>. British military technology brought a major victory.</p><p>With British warships poised to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, the officials negotiated on board HMS Cornwallis in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. On 29 August 1842 Sir Henry Pottinger for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Keying, Yilibu and Niu Jian for the Qing signed the treaty of thirteen articles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty's fundamental purpose was to change the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> system of foreign trade, in force since 1760. Article V abolished the monopoly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohong">Cohong</a> merchants and their Thirteen Factories, and the ports of Xiamen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningbo">Ningbo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> opened alongside Canton, where foreign merchants could trade with anyone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> could send consuls to the ports, and trade there was to be subject to fixed tariffs agreed between the two governments.</p><p>The Qing government was to pay 27 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> dollars in instalments over three years: 6 million for the opium Lin Zexu had confiscated, 6 million for the Ransom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a>, 3 million for the debts of Canton merchants to British merchants and 12 million for the cost of the war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a> was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> as a crown colony, in perpetuity, to give British traders a harbour. The treaty did not mention the opium trade, and the British asked the Chinese to legalise it themselves, which they refused.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Daoguang Emperor ratified the treaty on 27 October and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> on 28 December, and the exchange took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> on 26 June 1843. Because the treaty was brief, a supplementary Treaty of the Bogue followed in 1843, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> extraterritorial rights and making its citizens in the treaty ports exempt from Chinese law. Together with the treaties of 1843, 1858 and 1860 it ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> system and set a framework for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s foreign trade that lasted almost a hundred years.</p><p>Chinese nationalists later called it an unequal treaty, since its terms were all concessions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, among them extraterritoriality and fixed tariffs, and counted it the start of a century of humiliation. Joanna Waley-Cohen writes that the Qing did not at the time regard it as very important, since it resembled an earlier treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>. Extraterritoriality was not formally abolished until 1943.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Nanking” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Anglo-Sikh Wars brought the Punjab, and the First Opium War, fought to keep the opium trade with Qing China, gained five Chinese ports and Hong Kong under the <b>Treaty of Nanjing</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The First Opium War of 1839–1842 exposed the inadequacy of Qing forces against industrialised European militaries: a British fleet with modern artillery and steam-powered warships such as the iron steamer Nemesis overcame Qing coastal and Yangtze defences and forced the <b>Treaty of Nanjing</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The First Opium War (1839–1842) had ended in the <b>Treaty of Nanjing</b>, which ceded Hong Kong Island to Britain and opened five ports to foreign trade.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>British forces occupied Hong Kong Island in January 1841 during the First Opium War, and the <b>Treaty of Nanking</b> ceded it to Britain in 1842.</blockquote><a href="/state/hong-kong-british">Hong Kong (British)</a></li><li><blockquote>After Shanghai was opened to foreign trade by the <b>Treaty of Nanking</b>, a British settlement was laid out along the Bund in 1845 and an American one at Hongkou in 1848.</blockquote><a href="/state/shanghai-international-settlement">Shanghai International Settlement</a></li></ul></section>
